of this is sounding like it makes sense and

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.439
I don't actually know anything about economics.

00:01:31.439 --> 00:01:34.979
So I figured, Hey, I might ask an adult who
does.

00:01:34.979 --> 00:01:35.979
Yeah.

00:01:35.979 --> 00:01:41.790
So the first thing I would do is go to Google
and type in debunking minimum wage price floor.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:42.790
Okay.

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:45.350
And you will find a way, wealth of information
about that.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:53.700
It is true that in a classical economics framework
you can put a very convincing looking graph

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:58.880
together, which will make it appear as though
that is the effect of a price floor.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.790
The reality is we are almost at full employment.

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:07.330
We continue to be almost at full employment,
even in places that have 1112 and $15 minimum

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:08.330
wages.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:12.660
And what you have to understand is that there
is a very particular in, well some people

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.550
are maybe or are acting in good faith, but
there is a very particular bad faith incentive

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:22.819
to make these arguments by people who just
don't want businesses regulated and they want

00:02:22.819 --> 00:02:27.370
businesses to be able to pay three, four,
$5 an hour.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:35.980
And the fact that you can have such an unequal
and problematic, uh, economy that people would

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:41.330
be willing to work for three or $4 an hour
doesn't mean that it's the right thing to

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:43.250
do or what we should be doing.

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:44.510
And that's the important thing.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:51.560
Yes, if you remove the minimum wage, you will
see a company's paying less and people accepting

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.379
it and being willing to accept that.

00:02:53.379 --> 00:02:55.950
But the longterm implications of that are
not good.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:59.420
The, the long term implications of that on
crime are not good.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:04.110
The longterm implications of that on government
benefits spending on welfare programs aren't

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:05.110
good.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:09.150
So you need to zoom out in order to really
understand the context of those talking points.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:10.890
Oh, okay.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:14.860
Um, and then my other question was about health
care.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:21.350
One argument that was made is that because
we have health insurance, it allows companies

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:26.950
to charge us really high prices and if we
didn't have like a health insurance base and

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:34.569
instead people paid like out of pocket themselves,
um, it would drive prices down or something

00:03:34.569 --> 00:03:38.490
because these companies would still have to
sell their products to people and if they

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:43.580
can afford less, uh, or something, it would
drive prices down.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:44.580
Yep.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.890
That's that, that's a total free market solution.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:53.670
And that might work, uh, when there, when
demand is not as inelastic as it is in healthcare,

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.099
but healthcare is different than it.

00:03:56.099 --> 00:04:01.209
Healthcare should not be treated as a normal
market because basically if you don't need

00:04:01.209 --> 00:04:07.000
a hip replacement, you don't care whether
a hip replacement costs a dollar or $1,000

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:08.569
or $100,000.

00:04:08.569 --> 00:04:13.260
If you do need a hip replacement, you're in
a relatively small group, right?

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:15.780
It's still not everybody needs hip replacements.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:22.760
And all of a sudden what it costs has no impact
whatsoever on how badly you need that hip

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:23.760
replacement.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.510
And this is the reason why those sort of free
market lezafaire ideas simply shouldn't even

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:30.820
be in the picture when we're talking about
healthcare.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:34.700
And most western developed countries have
already figured that out.

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:40.040
